\section{Regions and the noise floor across heads}\label{sec:S6}

Unless stated otherwise, $D$ is the dense optically pumped magnetometer (OPM) array's detectability relative to Neuromag's << F.g2_squid_channels >> channels in dB (Section~\ref{sec:S1}), the area-weighted median over the cortical targets of a head or region (both hemispheres), with sensor plus brain noise. The helmets are those of Section~\ref{sec:S5}.

\subsection{Regions by head}\label{sec:S6.1}

In the fixed adult helmet at top contact, the dense array was ahead in every region of every head ($D$ << F.regh_top_vs_combined_cells_range >> dB over the cells of Fig.~\ref{fig:S9}A), and a smaller head exceeded the adult's value for the same region in << F.regh_top_vs_combined_smaller_cells_above_adult_count >> of << F.regh_top_vs_combined_smaller_cells_n >> cells. Of the six lobes, the temporal lobe gained most in the infant templates (<< F.regh_infant2yr_top_vs_combined_temporal >>, << F.regh_infant18mo_top_vs_combined_temporal >> and << F.regh_infant12mo_top_vs_combined_temporal >> dB at << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>, << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >> and << F.g3b_infant12mo_age_months >> months, against << F.regh_adult_top_vs_combined_temporal >> dB in the adult).

The parahippocampal and mesial temporal rows, the adult's lowest temporal rows (<< F.regh_adult_top_vs_combined_parahippocampal >> and << F.regh_adult_top_vs_combined_mesial_temporal >> dB), were higher in every template and scaled adult. The mesial temporal group reached << F.regh_templates_scaled_top_vs_combined_mesial_temporal_range >> dB in the templates and scaled adults but << F.regh_children_top_vs_combined_mesial_temporal_range >> dB in the children, child C (<< F.regh_child_c_top_vs_combined_mesial_temporal >> dB) lying below the adult. The cingulate was the lowest row in every head (<< F.regh_adult_top_vs_combined_cingulate >> dB in the adult, << F.regh_smaller_top_vs_combined_cingulate_range >> dB in the smaller heads).

The peak-channel signal-to-noise ratio (SNR; sensor plus brain noise) gave a different picture. Against the magnetometers alone, the dense array was ahead in every head (<< F.g3b_adult_dense_vs_mag_ib_peak_d >> dB in the adult at top contact, << F.g3b_smaller_dense_vs_mag_ib_peak_d_range >> dB in the smaller heads). Against Neuromag's best channel, Neuromag led in the adult (<< F.g3b_adult_dense_vs_combined_ib_peak_d >> dB) and in << F.g3b_smaller_dense_vs_combined_ib_peak_d_negative_count >> of the << F.g4_n_smaller_heads >> smaller heads (<< F.g3b_smaller_dense_vs_combined_ib_peak_d_range >> dB), the children among them (<< F.g3b_child_a_dense_vs_combined_ib_peak_d >>, << F.g3b_child_b_dense_vs_combined_ib_peak_d >> and << F.g3b_child_c_dense_vs_combined_ib_peak_d >> dB in A, B and C).

In the helmet scaled with the head (lobes only; Fig.~\ref{fig:S9}B), the regional gain was smaller, by how much depending on the reference: << F.regh_cfx_vs_combined_smaller_lobe_cells_at_or_below_adult_count >> of the << F.regh_cfx_vs_combined_smaller_lobe_cells_n >> lobe cells of the smaller heads were at or below the adult's in its own scaled helmet, and << F.regh_cfx_vs_combined_smaller_lobe_cells_at_or_below_adult_top_count >> at or below the adult's at top contact (<< F.regh_cfx_vs_combined_templates_scaled_lobe_cells_at_or_below_adult_top_count >> of << F.regh_cfx_vs_combined_templates_scaled_lobe_cells_n >> for the templates and scaled adults, << F.regh_cfx_vs_combined_children_lobe_cells_at_or_below_adult_top_count >> of << F.regh_cfx_vs_combined_children_lobe_cells_n >> for the children), counting point estimates without a tolerance. The children's lobes ranged from << F.regh_children_cfx_vs_combined_lobes_range >> dB in the scaled helmet.

In the helmet fitted at the adult's gap (Table~\ref{tab:S8}), the scaled adults kept the adult's ordering (frontal, precentral and superior temporal cortex highest; cingulate and insula lowest), and the cingulate was the lowest row in every head except the children, where the insula lay just below it. The templates and children reordered the upper rows: parahippocampal and mesial temporal rows were high in the << F.g3b_infant2yr_age_months >>- and << F.g3b_infant18mo_age_months >>-month templates and in child B, and the occipital lobe was highest in child C.

The children differed from the adult by region in both directions. Child B was below the adult in the frontal lobe (<< F.rev_cgap_child_b_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_frontal >> dB) and the precentral gyrus (<< F.rev_cgap_child_b_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_precentral >> dB) but above it in the parahippocampal (<< F.rev_cgap_child_b_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_parahippocampal >> dB) and occipital (<< F.rev_cgap_child_b_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_occipital >> dB) cortex, and child C was below it in the entorhinal cortex (<< F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_entorhinal >> dB) but above it in the occipital lobe (<< F.rev_cgap_child_c_fitted_dense_vs_combined_ib_d_minus_adult_occipital >> dB).

The cortical maps (Fig.~\ref{fig:S10}; main text, Fig. 8C) show $D$ largest on the lateral convexity, most on gyral crowns, with medial surfaces near zero. By region, then, the ordering changed with the helmet; the relative weight of helmet and region was not quantified. Heat maps and cortical maps show point estimates, and the pediatric intervals represent within-anatomy resampling only.

